    "created_at": "2020-06-11T03:09:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-06-11T03:09:41Z",
    "body": "But it's definitely not correct that we're displaying an error message instead of the PHP version here. \r\n\r\nIf the version is unobtainable due to error, we should either hide the PHP module or display an error symbol, not spew garbage onto the user's prompt.\r\n\r\nThe real question is whether this is an upstream issue or one with us. The full output of the PHP command is evidently:\r\n\r\n```\r\nDeprecated: Directive 'track_errors' is deprecated in Unknown on line 0\r\n7.4.1\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut PHP *doesn't* indicate an error exit status (or else our internal command executor would have caught it and `None`'d out the module). Given that we were trying to print the version number, is this correct behaviour on the part of the PHP program?\r\n\r\nIf this is correct, then we need to implement some sort of additional filtration on our end. If it's wrong, we need to refer it upstream.\r\n\r\nMy suspicion is that the error message is printed on stderr, while the version is printed on stdout, but because we smash them together in `internal_cmd_exec`, we can't separate them.",
